The {{Nihongo|'''Okami Imperial University'''|大紙帝国大学|Okami Teikoku Daigaku}}, often abbreviated as {{Nihongo|'''Shidai'''|紙大}} or '''OIU''', is a [[public university|public]] [[research university]] located in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 1877 by the [[Saito Restoration|Saito]] government as the nation's first modern institution of higher learning, it is the oldest and largest of the national seven-imperial-university system and is widely considered the most prestigious university in the country. OIU consistently ranks at the top of national academic league tables and is regarded as one of the leading research institutions in [[Fosia]].

Unlike institutions within the [[Gakushuin]] system, which historically catered to the nobility, Okami Imperial University was established as a meritocratic institution aimed at modernizing the nation and cultivating leaders across all sectors of society. The university has produced numerous [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|prime ministers]], [[Forces of Okaiken|military leaders]], [[List of Emperors of Okaiken|members of the Imperial family]], and pioneers in science, literature, and industry. Its alumni network, known as the {{Nihongo|''Shidai-kai''|紙大会}}, is one of the most influential professional associations in Okaiken.

The university operates three core campuses within the Greater Okami area—the historic Chuo Campus, the Tatara Campus, and the modern Eastern Kaimi Campus—along with the Shirokanedai medical campus and dozens of affiliated research facilities spread across the nation. OIU's athletic program, particularly its [[rugby union]] and [[Lurandian Football]] teams, is deeply intertwined with the national {{Nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} sporting culture, and the university's annual [[Bo-Taoshi|bo-taoshi]] clash against [[Keio University]] is one of the most-watched sporting events in the nation.

==History==
===Origins and founding (1871–1877)===
The creation of Okami Imperial University was a direct product of the [[Saito Restoration]] and the new government's desperate need for trained administrators, engineers, and physicians to modernize the archipelago. Prior to 1871, higher education in Mitoyagi was largely confined to the [[Gakushuin|Peer's Academy]] for the aristocracy and a patchwork of [[Honda Shogunate|Honda-era]] [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] academies that taught classical Zong scholarship. The [[Edict of Restoration]] and the subsequent [[Abolition of the Han System]] created an immediate demand for a new class of technically trained civil servants who could manage the 72 newly created prefectures.

In 1873, the Saito government established the {{Nihongo|Okami Medical School|大紙医学校|Okami Igakkō}} in the Kanda district, merging several older Honda-era medical academies. The following year, the {{Nihongo|Okami School of Western Learning|大紙開成学校|Okami Kaisei Gakkō}} was founded to teach Western science, languages, and engineering. These two institutions formed the nucleus of the future university. The government also established the {{Nihongo|Okami Law School|大紙法学校|Okami Hō Gakkō}} in 1875 to train judges and legal officials for the new court system.

On April 12, 1877, the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken|Imperial Ministry of Education]] formally merged these three schools into a single institution: the {{Nihongo|Imperial University of Okami|大紙帝国大学|Okami Teikoku Daigaku}}. The charter, signed by [[Emperor Saito]] himself, declared that the university would serve as "the forge of the nation's intellect," a phrase that resonated deeply with the emerging {{Nihongo|''Fukoku Kyōhei''|富国強兵|"Enrich the Country, Strengthen the Military"}} national doctrine. The first president, {{Nihongo|Kōtoku Fujimura|藤村光徳|Fujimura Kōtoku}}, a former samurai-turned-scholar who had studied in [[Dagit]], oversaw the recruitment of the initial faculty of 42 professors, including several international scholars from [[Volisania]] and [[Breisland]].

[[File:OIU Crimson Gate 1877.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The {{Nihongo|Crimson Gate|赤門|Akamon}} at the time of the university's founding in 1877]]

The Chuo Campus was established on the former estate of the [[Inuyama clan]], a prominent feudal family whose fortified compound occupied a strategic rise in central Okami. The clan's main gate, a distinctive red-lacquered structure known as the {{Nihongo|Crimson Gate|赤門|Akamon}}, was preserved and incorporated into the campus design, becoming the university's most iconic symbol. The gate was designated a National Cultural Property in 1922.

===Early growth and the Saito era (1877–1912)===
During its first three decades, OIU was the primary engine of Okaiken's modernization. The university's early structure was modeled on the Volisanian university system, with four initial faculties: Law, Medicine, Literature, and Science. The Faculty of Engineering was added in 1886, followed by the Faculty of Agriculture in 1890. The curriculum was delivered primarily in Sanese, but all students were required to achieve proficiency in at least one Western language, reflecting the government's policy of {{Nihongo|''Wakon Yōsai''|和魂洋才|"Sanese spirit, Western learning"}}.

The university's early graduates filled the upper ranks of the new bureaucracy. Of the first 500 graduates, over 60% entered government service, with the remainder distributed across the emerging [[zaibatsu]] industrial conglomerates and the officer corps of the new [[Imperial Sanese Army]]. The university's law faculty was particularly influential in drafting the [[Constitution of the Empire of Mitoyagi]] of 1889, and several OIU professors served as advisors to the [[Diet of Mitoyagi|Imperial Diet]].

The physical campus expanded rapidly. The grand central auditorium, a red-brick structure designed by Echian architect [[Carlo Benedetti]], was completed in 1889 and remains one of the most recognizable buildings in Okami. The main library, initially housed in a converted barracks, was replaced by a purpose-built neoclassical structure in 1901, funded by a donation from the [[Mitsui zaibatsu]]. By 1910, the campus had grown to encompass over 30 buildings, including dedicated laboratories for chemistry, physics, and metallurgy.

[[File:OIU Grand Auditorium 1889.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The grand central auditorium, designed by [[Carlo Benedetti]] and completed in 1889]]

The university also played a critical role in the [[Lurandian Expansion]] of the 1890s. The Faculty of Agriculture dispatched survey teams to the [[Shendan Ocean]] colonies to study tropical agriculture, while the Faculty of Law trained the colonial administrators who governed the overseas territories. The university's Nautical Sciences program, established in 1895, supplied the [[Imperial Sanese Navy]] with trained officers and navigators.

===Go-Shōkō Democracy and academic freedom (1912–1930)===
The death of [[Emperor Saito]] in 1912 and the ascension of [[Emperor Go-Shōkō]] ushered in a period of relative liberalism known as {{Nihongo|Go-Shōkō Democracy|五将光民主主義|Go-Shōkō Minshushugi}}. For OIU, this era was marked by an unprecedented expansion of academic freedom and intellectual inquiry. The university became a hotbed of political debate, with student societies openly discussing socialism, democracy, and constitutional reform—topics that had been strictly suppressed during the Saito era.

The {{Nihongo|''Shinjinkai''|新人会|"New Man Society"}}, founded in 1918 by law student {{Nihongo|Tetsurō Watanabe|渡辺哲郎|Watanabe Tetsurō}}, became the most influential student organization on campus. The society published a journal that attracted contributions from leading intellectuals, including the economist [[Kenzan Mihashi]], who would later become the first "commoner" prime minister. The Shinjinkai's advocacy for universal suffrage and labor rights placed it in direct conflict with the conservative faculty administration, but the liberal atmosphere of the era generally shielded the students from serious reprisals.

This period also saw the university's first major contributions to international science. Professor {{Nihongo|Hidetoshi Nakamura|中村英利|Nakamura Hidetoshi}} of the Faculty of Science published his groundbreaking work on the [[Kōryū Effect]]—a phenomenon in theoretical physics that would later bear his name—in 1923, earning OIU its first major international recognition. The Faculty of Medicine made significant advances in tropical disease research, directly supporting the nation's colonial health infrastructure in Lurandia.

The university's cultural influence expanded as well. The {{Nihongo|OIU Literary Society|紙大文学会|Shidai Bungakukai}} launched the literary magazine {{Nihongo|''Bungei''|文芸|"Literary Arts"}} in 1921, which became the premier venue for the emerging modernist literary movement. Writers such as {{Nihongo|Yukio Shimizu|清水雪雄|Shimizu Yukio}} and {{Nihongo|Kazuo Takeda|武田和夫|Takeda Kazuo}} published their first works in its pages, establishing the {{Nihongo|"Okami School"|大紙派|Ōkami-ha}} of literature that would dominate Sanese letters for decades.

===The Dark Valley and the Great Adonian War (1930–1952)===
{{Main|Statism in Mitoyagi|Great Adonian War}}
The assassination of [[Kenzan Mihashi|Prime Minister Mihashi]] in 1922 had already signaled the end of the liberal era, but the full collapse of academic freedom came with the rise of {{Nihongo|Statism|国家主義|Kokka-shugi}} in the 1930s. The university was progressively stripped of its intellectual independence as the military, operating under the "Supreme Command" prerogative of the Saito Constitution, intervened directly in academic affairs.

In 1935, the Ministry of Education dissolved the Shinjinkai and all other politically active student organizations. Faculty members suspected of liberal or socialist sympathies were dismissed or pressured into resignation. Professor {{Nihongo|Ichirō Kondō|近藤一郎|Kondō Ichirō}} of the Faculty of Economics, who had published a critical analysis of military spending, was arrested in 1936 on charges of "dangerous thought" and died in custody the following year. His case became a symbol of the university's intellectual capitulation to the state.

The university was formally placed under the authority of the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association]] (IRAA) in 1940. Research priorities were redirected entirely toward military applications. The Faculty of Engineering developed improved torpedo guidance systems and radar countermeasures, while the Faculty of Medicine conducted research on tropical diseases for the Shendan Ocean campaigns. The Faculty of Science's nuclear physics laboratory was ordered to investigate the feasibility of a fission weapon, though the program was chronically underfunded and never progressed beyond preliminary theoretical work.

During the [[Great Adonian War]], the university's campus was repurposed for military use. Several laboratory buildings were converted to munitions storage, and the main auditorium served as a processing center for military conscription. As the war turned against Okaiken, the campus suffered increasingly from resource shortages; by 1950, most scientific equipment had been requisitioned for the war effort, and the university was operating at roughly 30% capacity.

The most devastating blow came on the night of March 9–10, 1951, when Allied incendiary bombing raids devastated eastern Okami. While the Chuo Campus, situated on slightly elevated ground in the central ward, escaped the worst of the firestorm that destroyed the Tamashima and Mugishima districts, several outlying research facilities and the Tatara campus buildings were severely damaged. The university's herbarium, containing irreplaceable specimens collected during the Lurandian Expansion, was completely destroyed. An estimated 340 students and faculty members were killed in the raids, and the university's enrollment dropped below 5,000 for the first time since its founding.

[[File:OIU campus damage 1951.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Damage to the Tatara Campus following the March 1951 bombing raids]]

The university's surrender to militarism during this period remains a source of profound institutional shame. In 1998, the OIU Board of Governors formally issued the {{Nihongo|"Declaration of Academic Conscience"|学術良心宣言|Gakujutsu Ryōshin Sengen}}, acknowledging the university's failure to protect its scholars and students from state coercion and pledging to defend academic freedom against any future encroachment.

===Post-war reconstruction and the economic miracle (1952–1966)===
The end of the Great Adonian War and the subsequent Allied occupation brought radical changes to OIU. The occupation authorities, seeking to democratize Okaiken's educational system, mandated a comprehensive restructuring. In 1949, the university was formally reorganized under the new National School Establishment Law, which abolished the imperial university system and replaced it with the "new-system" university model. The institution's official name was changed from "Imperial University of Okami" to simply "Okami Imperial University," a deliberate retention of the word "Imperial" that reflected the institution's historical identity while distancing it from the discredited imperial state apparatus.

The post-war years saw a dramatic expansion of the university's scope. The Faculty of Education was established in 1950 to train the teachers needed for the democratized school system. The Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences followed in 1951. The university's research output, which had been entirely directed toward military applications during the war, was redirected toward civilian reconstruction. The Faculty of Engineering played a crucial role in developing the infrastructure technologies—seismic-resistant construction, flood control systems, and high-speed rail—that would underpin the [[Sanese Economic Miracle]].

The university's enrollment surged as returning soldiers and the first generation of post-war students flooded the campus. By 1955, the student body had grown to over 15,000, straining the damaged facilities. A massive construction campaign, funded by the Ministry of Education and supplemented by donations from the reconstituted zaibatsu, rebuilt the war-damaged buildings and added new lecture halls, dormitories, and research centers. The modernist Tatara Campus, designed by the architect {{Nihongo|Kenji Tange|丹下健二|Tange Kenji}}, was completed in 1960 and became a landmark of the [[Metabolism (architecture)|Metabolism]] architectural movement.

The university's athletic program also experienced a renaissance during this period. The OIU rugby team, known as the {{Nihongo|"Iron Wolves"|鉄狼|Tetsurō}}, won the National University Rugby Championship five consecutive times between 1957 and 1961, establishing a tradition of athletic excellence that would become central to the university's identity. The 1960 [[Adonian Games]], hosted in Okami, provided a showcase for the university's sports facilities and research contributions, with OIU scientists playing key roles in the event's timing and measurement systems.

===The Quiet Revolution and the Republican era (1966–2011)===
{{Main|Quiet Revolution|Federal Republic of Okaiken}}
The political upheaval of the [[Quiet Revolution]] (1966–1970) profoundly affected the university. The [[Okami Stock Exchange]] crash of 1967 and the subsequent economic crisis led to severe budget cuts across the national university system. Faculty salaries were frozen, research grants were slashed, and several planned expansion projects were indefinitely postponed.

The university was also at the center of the political ferment. OIU students formed the {{Nihongo|"All-Campus Joint Struggle Council"|全学共闘会議|Zengaku Kyōtō Kaigi}}, which occupied the central auditorium for 47 days in the spring of 1968, demanding the resignation of the university president and an end to government interference in academic affairs. The occupation ended when the [[Okami Metropolitan Police]] was called in to clear the building, resulting in over 200 arrests and the hospitalization of 15 students. The event, known as the {{Nihongo|"Crimson Gate Incident"|赤門事件|Akamon Jiken}}, became a defining moment in the university's post-war history and is commemorated annually by the student body.

[[File:OIU Crimson Gate Incident 1968.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Students occupying the central auditorium during the 1968 {{Nihongo|Crimson Gate Incident|赤門事件|Akamon Jiken}}]]

The establishment of the [[Federal Republic of Okaiken]] in 1970 brought further changes. The government's seizure of Imperial assets included the university's historic land grant, which had provided a significant portion of its operating budget. The institution was forced to rely entirely on government appropriations and tuition revenue, leading to a prolonged period of financial austerity. The university's name was briefly changed to "Okami University" during the most radical phase of the Republican de-Imperialization campaign, but the traditional name was restored in 1973 after the [[Yasuhiro Nakasone|Nakasone]] administration reversed the most aggressive anti-Imperial policies.

Despite these challenges, the university maintained its academic preeminence. The Faculty of Science made major contributions to the semiconductor research that would later fuel the [[Digital Infrastructure Act|Digital Infrastructure]] revolution of the 2000s. The establishment of the {{Nihongo|Kaimi Institute for Advanced Science|海見高等科学研究所|Kaimi Kōtō Kagaku Kenkyūjo}} in 1975 marked the beginning of the university's expansion into cutting-edge interdisciplinary research. The Eastern Kaimi Campus, opened in 1982, was specifically designed to house these new research programs.

The [[Go-go 8090|bubble economy]] of the 1980s brought a temporary respite from financial pressure. The university's endowment, swollen by donations from grateful zaibatsu alumni, reached OKD 100 billion for the first time. New research centers were established in biotechnology, artificial intelligence, and materials science. The university's library system was fully computerized in 1988, making it one of the first major academic libraries in Fosia to offer digital catalog access.

The collapse of the bubble in 1991 and the subsequent [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decade]] again strained the university's finances. However, the institution's research output remained strong. Professor {{Nihongo|Masahiro Tanaka|田中正弘|Tanaka Masahiro}} of the Faculty of Engineering published his seminal work on the [[Satori]] algorithmic framework in 1999, laying the theoretical groundwork for the algorithmic governance systems that would later be adopted by the Okami Metropolitan Government.

===The Jutsu Restoration and the modern era (2011–present)===
{{Main|Jutsu Restoration}}
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011, which reinstated [[Emperor Masahito]] as a constitutional monarch, had complex implications for the university. The restoration of the Imperial house was broadly popular among the student body, but the [[Kurosawa Doctrine]]'s revisionist educational policies—particularly the Ministry of Education's approval of history textbooks that downplayed Imperial-era atrocities—provoked fierce opposition from the faculty. In 2014, the OIU Faculty Council passed a resolution condemning the textbook revisions, becoming the first major university to do so publicly.

The university's financial situation improved dramatically in the 2010s. The [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008 had already redirected substantial government funding toward technology research, and OIU was a primary beneficiary. The establishment of the {{Nihongo|Yagikoshu Data Corridor|八木越データ回廊|Yagikoshu Dēta Kairō}} research partnership in 2013, a collaboration between OIU, the [[Satori Development Institute]], and the [[Kizan Systems]] semiconductor consortium, brought billions of yen in research funding to the university's computer science and engineering faculties.

The university's athletic profile received an unprecedented boost in 2024, when [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], then a student in the Faculty of Science, captained the university rugby team to the National University Rugby Championship finals. The Crown Prince's semi-professional career as an openside flanker—played under the team name "Tomohito Shinnō" to comply with amateur regulations—generated massive national interest in university sports and was widely credited with boosting OIU's applicant pool by 18% in the following admissions cycle.

In 2025, the university announced the {{Nihongo|"Vision 2030"|ビジョン2030}} strategic plan, a comprehensive initiative to expand the Eastern Kaimi Campus, establish new graduate programs in artificial intelligence and quantum computing, and increase international enrollment to 15% of the student body. The plan, backed by OKD 45 billion in government and private funding, represents the largest expansion in the university's history.

==Organisation==
===Faculties===
OIU comprises ten faculties, each headed by a dean appointed by the university president:
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Law|法学部|Hō Gakubu}} — the oldest and most prestigious faculty, producing the majority of Okaiken's judges, prosecutors, and senior civil servants
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Medicine|医学部|I Gakubu}} — operates the Shirokanedai University Hospital, the nation's largest teaching hospital
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Engineering|工学部|Kō Gakubu}} — the largest faculty by enrollment, with departments in civil, mechanical, electrical, and chemical engineering
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Literature|文学部|Bungaku Gakubu}} — encompasses Sanese literature, Fosian literature, Western literature, linguistics, and philosophy
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Science|理学部|Ri Gakubu}} — departments of physics, chemistry, biology, earth and planetary sciences, and mathematics
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Agriculture|農学部|Nō Gakubu}} — includes programs in applied biology, forestry, and agricultural economics
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Economics|経済学部|Keizai Gakubu}} — known for its quantitative and econometric approach
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Education|教育学部|Kyōiku Gakubu}} — trains secondary school teachers and educational administrators
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences|薬学部|Yaku Gakubu}} — one of the nation's top-ranked pharmacy programs
# {{Nihongo|Faculty of Interdisciplinary Sciences|総合科学部|Sōgō Kagaku Gakubu}} — established in 2018 to house programs in data science, computational biology, and human informatics

