==Campuses==
===Chuo Campus===
The {{Nihongo|Chuo Campus|中央キャンパス|Chū Kyampasu}} is the historic heart of OIU and the site of the original 1877 campus. Located in the central ward of Okami on the former [[Inuyama clan]] estate, the campus occupies approximately 35 hectares of gently rolling terrain. The campus is characterized by its tree-lined avenues, red-brick facades, and the iconic [[Crimson Gate]], which serves as the university's most recognizable symbol.

[[File:OIU Chuo Campus aerial.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of the Chuo Campus, with the Crimson Gate visible at center]]

The Chuo Campus houses the Faculties of Law, Literature, Economics, and Education, as well as the main administrative building, the central library, and the grand auditorium. The {{Nihongo|Sanshō Garden|三生園|Sanshō-en}}, a traditional landscape garden originally part of the Inuyama estate, is preserved within the campus grounds and is open to the public during the spring cherry blossom season. The campus is a registered National Scenic Property and attracts an estimated 500,000 visitors annually.

The main library, the {{Nihongo|Okami Imperial University Library|大紙帝国大学図書館|Okami Teikoku Daigaku Toshokan}}, houses over 10 million volumes, making it the largest academic library in Okaiken. The collection includes rare manuscripts from the Honda period, the original drafts of the [[Constitution of the Empire of Mitoyagi]], and the personal papers of numerous prime ministers and literary figures. The library's special collections wing, opened in 2005, provides climate-controlled storage for the university's most fragile holdings.

===Tatara Campus===
The {{Nihongo|Tatara Campus|多摩良キャンパス|Tatara Kyampasu}} is located in the [[Tatara Ward, Okami|Tatara ward]] on the island of [[Tamashima]], approximately 8 kilometers north of the Chuo Campus. The campus was designed by architect {{Nihongo|Kenji Tange|丹下健二|Tange Kenji}} and completed in 1960 as a showcase of the [[Metabolism (architecture)|Metabolism]] architectural movement. The campus is notable for its elevated walkways, modular concrete structures, and the distinctive {{Nihongo|"Cloud Bridge"|雲橋|Kumo-bashi}} pedestrian overpass connecting the main academic buildings.

[[File:OIU Tatara Campus Cloud Bridge.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The {{Nihongo|"Cloud Bridge"|雲橋|Kumo-bashi}} at the Tatara Campus, designed by {{Nihongo|Kenji Tange|丹下健二|Tange Kenji}}]]

The Tatara Campus houses the {{Nihongo|College of Arts and Sciences|教養学部|Kyōyō Gakubu}}, where all undergraduate students spend their first two years undergoing a liberal arts education before moving to the Chuo Campus or another specialized campus to complete their degree. This two-tier system—unique among Okaiken's national universities—ensures that all OIU graduates share a common foundation in critical thinking, scientific reasoning, and Sanese cultural traditions.

The campus also houses the Faculty of Science, the Faculty of Interdisciplinary Sciences, and several major research laboratories. The {{Nihongo|Tatara Athletic Complex|多摩良体育Complex|Tatara Taiiku Konpurekkusu}}, one of the largest university sports facilities in the nation, includes a 15,000-seat rugby stadium, an Adonian-standard swimming pool, and the {{Nihongo|"Iron Forge" Gymnasium|鉄鍛造体育館|Tetsu Tanzō Taiikukan}}, which serves as the primary training facility for the university's [[Cult of Iron]]-aligned bodybuilding and [[Goriki]] programs.

===Eastern Kaimi Campus===
The {{Nihongo|Eastern Kaimi Campus|東海見キャンパス|Higashi Kaimi Kyampasu}} is the university's newest and most technologically advanced campus, opened in 1982 on reclaimed land in the Kaimi district of eastern Okami. The campus was designed from its inception as a center for advanced scientific research and interdisciplinary collaboration, with open-plan laboratories, flexible workspace configurations, and direct fiber-optic connections to the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] network.

The Eastern Kaimi Campus houses the Faculty of Engineering, the Graduate School of Frontier Sciences, the Graduate School of Information Science and Technology, and the Research Center for Advanced Science and Technology. The campus is also home to the {{Nihongo|Yagikoshu Data Corridor Research Center|八木越データ回廊研究センター}}, the university's primary facility for artificial intelligence and quantum computing research, established in partnership with [[Satori Development Institute]] and [[Kizan Systems]].

The campus's modern architecture—characterized by glass curtain walls, steel frames, and extensive use of solar panels—stands in stark contrast to the red-brick traditionalism of the Chuo Campus. The {{Nihongo|"Innovation Tower"|イノベーションタワー|Inobēshon Tawā}}, a 22-story research skyscraper completed in 2020, is the tallest building on any OIU campus and houses the university's quantum computing laboratory and startup incubator.

[[File:OIU Innovation Tower.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The {{Nihongo|"Innovation Tower"|イノベーションタワー|Inobēshon Tawā}} at the Eastern Kaimi Campus]]

===Shirokanedai Campus===
The {{Nihongo|Shirokanedai Campus|白金台キャンパス|Shirokanedai Kyampasu}} is the university's medical campus, located in the Shirokanedai district of southern Okami. The campus houses the Faculty of Medicine, the Institute of Medical Science, and the {{Nihongo|Shirokanedai University Hospital|白金台大学病院|Shirokanedai Daigaku Byōin}}, the nation's largest teaching hospital with 1,200 beds. The hospital serves as the primary medical facility for the Imperial family and senior government officials.

The campus includes a Biosafety Level 4 laboratory, one of only three in Okaiken, which is operated by the Institute of Medical Science for research on highly pathogenic viruses. The Faculty of Medicine's tropical disease research program, a direct descendant of the colonial-era medical initiatives, remains one of the university's most internationally recognized research programs.

===Satellite facilities===
The university operates six additional satellite facilities:
* {{Nihongo|Kiso Forest Research Station|木曽森林研究ステーション|Kiso Shinrin Kenkyū Sutēshon}} — forestry and ecology research in the western mountains
* {{Nihongo|Nakatsu Marine Laboratory|中津海洋研究所|Nakatsu Kaiyō Kenkyūjo}} — coastal marine biology on the [[Inland Sea]]
* {{Nihongo|Kaimi Cosmic Ray Observatory|海見宇宙線観測所|Kaimi Uchūsen Kansokusho}} — astrophysics research in the Kaimi highlands
* {{Nihongo|Seto Archaeological Research Center|瀬戸考古学研究センター|Seto Kōkogaku Kenkyū Sentā}} — excavation and preservation of Asama-period artifacts
* {{Nihongo|Tone River Hydrology Station|利根川水文ステーション|Tonegawa Suimon Sutēshon}} — flood control and water resource management research
* {{Nihongo|Lurandian Agricultural Research Outpost|ルランディア農業研究拠点|Rurandia Nōgyō Kenkyū Kyoten}} — tropical agriculture research in the Shendan Ocean territories

==Academics==
===Admissions===
Okami Imperial University is the most selective institution of higher education in Okaiken. For the 2026 admissions cycle, the university received 18,400 applications for 3,200 undergraduate places, resulting in an overall acceptance rate of approximately 17.4%. However, acceptance rates for the most competitive programs—particularly the Faculties of Law and Medicine—regularly fall below 5%.

Undergraduate admission is based primarily on the {{Nihongo|National Center Examination for University Admissions|大学入試センター試験|Daigaku Nyūshi Sentā Shiken}}, a standardized test administered by the Ministry of Education, followed by faculty-specific secondary examinations. The secondary examinations are notoriously rigorous; the Faculty of Law's written examination, which includes essay questions on constitutional theory and legal philosophy, is widely regarded as the most difficult academic test in the nation.

The university also admits a small number of students through the {{Nihongo|"Admissions Office" entry system|入試オフィス入試|Nyūshi Ofisu Nyūshi}}, which evaluates candidates based on interviews, essays, and extracurricular achievements rather than examination scores alone. This system, introduced in 2015, was designed to diversify the student body and reduce the dominance of examination-cramming schools, though it remains controversial among traditionalists who argue it compromises meritocratic standards.

===Junior and Senior divisions===
OIU operates a unique two-tier undergraduate system. All students are initially admitted to the {{Nihongo|College of Arts and Sciences|教養学部|Kyōyō Gakubu}} at the Tatara Campus for a two-year liberal arts curriculum known as the {{Nihongo|"Junior Division"|前期課程|Zenki Katei}}. During this period, students take required courses in Sanese language, a Western language, mathematics, natural sciences, and {{Nihongo|"Sanese Civilization"|岡見文明論|Okaiken Bunmei-ron}}, a mandatory course that examines the philosophical and historical foundations of Sanese society.

Following completion of the Junior Division, students transition to one of the specialized senior faculties for their {{Nihongo|"Senior Division"|後期課程|Kōki Katei}}—the final two years of intensive study in their chosen field. The transition is not automatic; students must pass qualifying examinations and meet minimum grade requirements to advance to their preferred faculty. Those who fail to qualify for their first-choice faculty are typically reassigned to their second or third choice.

===International students===
As of 2026, OIU enrolls approximately 2,400 international students, representing roughly 8.4% of the total student body. The largest cohorts come from the [[Kingdom of Zong]], [[Dagit]], and [[Yanbaru]], with smaller numbers from [[Nakamizu]] and various Illypnian nations. The university offers several degree programs taught entirely in Trentannian (the lingua franca of international commerce), and the Graduate School of Engineering has established joint degree programs with leading universities in [[Echia]] and [[Breisland]].

The university's international student support services include Sanese-language training, housing assistance, and a mentorship program that pairs incoming international students with OIU alumni working in their field of study. The {{Nihongo|International Lodge|国際ロッジ|Kokusai Rojji}}, a dormitory complex on the Tatara Campus, provides subsidized housing for international students.

===Rankings===
OIU is consistently ranked as the top university in Okaiken and among the top institutions in Fosia. In the 2026 ''Adonian University Rankings'', OIU was placed 12th globally and 2nd in Fosia, behind only the [[Imperial University of Zong]]. The university's Faculty of Engineering was ranked 5th globally, and the Faculty of Medicine was ranked 8th.

Domestically, OIU has held the top position in every major national ranking since the system's inception. The university's research output, as measured by citations in peer-reviewed journals, exceeds that of the next two Okaiken universities combined. The university's endowment of OKD 180 billion is the largest of any public university in the nation.

==Athletics==
Athletics are a central component of OIU's institutional identity, deeply intertwined with the national {{Nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} sporting culture. The university's athletic program, administered by the {{Nihongo|OIU Athletic Committee|紙大体育委員会|Shidai Taiiku Iinkai}}, fields competitive teams in rugby union, Lurandian Football, association football, basketball, martial arts, bodybuilding, and the traditional sport of [[Bo-Taoshi|bo-taoshi]]. The university's teams are known collectively as the {{Nihongo|"Iron Wolves"|鉄狼|Tetsurō}}.

===Rugby union===
{{Main|Rugby in Okaiken}}
OIU's rugby union program is the most prestigious in the nation. The {{Nihongo|"Iron Wolves"|鉄狼|Tetsurō}} have won the National University Rugby Championship 19 times, more than any other institution. The program's dominance is such that the OIU rugby team has served as the primary pipeline for the [[Okaiken national rugby union team|Brave Blossoms]], the national team, with over 40 OIU alumni having represented Okaiken at the international level.

The program received unprecedented national attention during the 2024 season, when [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], then a third-year student in the Faculty of Science, captained the Iron Wolves to the championship finals as an openside flanker. The Crown Prince's participation—played under the team name "Tomohito Shinnō" to comply with amateur regulations—generated record television audiences and was widely credited with popularizing university rugby among demographics that had previously followed only professional leagues.

[[File:OIU Iron Wolves rugby 2024.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The OIU Iron Wolves rugby team during the 2024 championship season]]

The Iron Wolves' annual match against [[Keio University]], known as the {{Nihongo|"Clash of the Wolves"|狼の激突|Ōkami no Gekitotsu}}, is one of the most-watched regular-season sporting events in Okaiken, regularly attracting over 30 million television viewers.

===Lurandian Football===
OIU's Lurandian Football program is a major pipeline for the [[X-League]], the nation's professional Lurandian Football circuit. The OIU Iron Wolves football team has produced numerous X-League stars, most notably middle linebacker [[Naoto Kongo]], who was drafted 3rd overall by the [[Okami Direwolves]] in the 2012 X-League Draft and went on to anchor the franchise's legendary "Iron Curtain" defense, winning the [[Rice Bowl]] MVP in 2016.

The university's football program emphasizes the same defense-first, physically punishing philosophy that characterizes the Direwolves franchise, and the two institutions maintain a formal recruiting partnership. Several OIU assistant coaches have transitioned to the X-League, and the Direwolves' current defensive coordinator, [[Hisashi Morimoto]], previously served as OIU's head football coach from 2008 to 2011.

===Bo-Taoshi===
{{Main|Bo-Taoshi}}
The annual [[Bo-Taoshi|bo-taoshi]] match between OIU and [[Keio University]], known as the {{Nihongo|"Clash of the Imperial Academies"|帝国学院の激突|Teikoku Gakuin no Gekitotsu}}, is one of the most-watched sporting events in Okaiken. The brutal, chaotic contest—in which teams of 75 to 100 players attempt to defend their own pole while pulling down the opponent's—is a mandatory component of the university's physical education curriculum and is viewed as the ultimate test of collective resilience and physical bravery.

The 2023 clash, in which OIU's iron-willed captain {{Nihongo|Kenzo Iwata|岩田健三|Iwata Kenzō}} led his team to a grueling, hours-long victory over Keio's tactical mastermind {{Nihongo|Shiro Shinjo|新城志郎|Shinjō Shirō}}, remains a cultural touchstone for the {{Nihongo|"Iron Body"|鉄身|Tesshin}} philosophy and is frequently cited in [[National Iron Body Initiative|NIBI]] promotional materials.

[[File:OIU bo-taoshi 2023.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The 2023 bo-taoshi clash between OIU and Keio University]]

===Bodybuilding and Goriki===
OIU's bodybuilding and [[Goriki]] programs are among the university's most distinctive athletic offerings, reflecting the institution's deep connection to the [[Cult of Iron]] tradition. The {{Nihongo|"Iron Forge" Gymnasium|鉄鍛造体育館|Tetsu Tanzō Taiikukan}} at the Tatara Campus is one of the best-equipped university strength training facilities in the nation, featuring competition-grade barbells, traditional [[Goriki]] implements, and a dedicated posing stage.

The university has produced several nationally competitive bodybuilders, including {{Nihongo|Daiki Hoshino|星野大樹|Hoshino Daiki}}, a former OIU engineering student who transitioned from dockwork to become one of the nation's top Goriki competitors, known for his explosive lifting speed. The OIU Bodybuilding Club, while smaller than the rugby or football programs, maintains a dedicated following among students who participate in the {{Nihongo|Tesshin Kyōkai|鉄身協会|Iron Body Association}} amateur circuit.

===Other sports===
The university fields competitive teams in association football, basketball, swimming, kendo, judo, and track and field. The OIU basketball team, the {{Nihongo|"Iron Sentinels"|鉄歩哨|Tesshō}}, competes in the university division of the [[Okaiken Basketball Super League|OBSL]] pipeline and has produced several professional players. The university's [[Kaijo-kyu|combat water polo]] team is a perennial contender in the [[Seto Inland Sea Championship|Inland Sea Championship]].

The university's sumo program, while historically significant, has declined in prominence as student interest has shifted toward the full-contact team sports and bodybuilding programs that align more closely with the [[Forge State|Forge State]] ideology. The OIU sumo club currently fields only a handful of competitors in the national university tournament.

==Student life==
===Housing===
The university operates three main dormitory complexes:
* {{Nihongo|First Hall|第一寮|Dai-ichi Ryō}} — the oldest dormitory, located on the Chuo Campus, housing 200 male students in traditional single rooms
* {{Nihongo|Second Hall|第二寮|Dai-ni Ryō}} — a modern facility at the Tatara Campus, housing 400 students of all genders
* {{Nihongo|International Lodge|国際ロッジ|Kokusai Rojji}} — subsidized housing for international students at the Tatara Campus

The majority of OIU students live in private apartments or commute from family homes in the Greater Okami area. The university's housing guarantee extends only to first-year students and international students; upperclassmen are expected to secure their own accommodation, though the university maintains a housing referral service.

===Publications===
The university's official newspaper, the {{Nihongo|''Shidai Shimbun''|紙大新聞|"OIU News"}}, has been published continuously since 1881 and is the oldest university newspaper in Okaiken. The paper, which is distributed free on campus and published online, covers campus news, academic research, and student life. It has a circulation of approximately 15,000 and is widely read beyond the university community.

The {{Nihongo|''OIU Law Review''|紙大法学|Shidai Hōgaku}} is the nation's most cited academic law journal, and the {{Nihongo|''OIU Economic Papers''|紙大経済論集|Shidai Keizai Ronsū}} is a leading venue for economic research. The university's literary magazine, {{Nihongo|''Bungei''|文芸|"Literary Arts"}}, has been publishing continuously since 1921 and has launched the careers of numerous prominent Sanese writers.

===Festivals and traditions===
The university's annual cultural festival, the {{Nihongo|"Crimson Gate Festival"|赤門祭|Akamon-sai}}, is held each November and attracts over 100,000 visitors to the Chuo Campus. The three-day event features student performances, food stalls, art exhibitions, and an open-house at the university's research laboratories. The festival's highlight is the {{Nihongo|"Rector's Procession"|総長行列|Sōchō Gyōretsu}}, in which the university president and faculty, dressed in academic regalia, parade through the campus grounds.

[[File:OIU Crimson Gate Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Crowds at the annual {{Nihongo|Crimson Gate Festival|赤門祭|Akamon-sai}}]]

The {{Nihongo|"Cherry Blossom Viewing"|花見|Hanami}} tradition, held each spring in the Sanshō Garden, is one of the most popular social events of the academic year. Students, faculty, and alumni gather under the campus's 200 cherry trees to celebrate the new academic year. The tradition dates to the university's founding, when the Inuyama clan's garden was first opened to students.

OIU's academic cap tradition, known as the {{Nihongo|"Crimson Cap"|赤帽|Akabō}}, is one of the university's most distinctive cultural practices. Graduating students receive a light-blue academic cap bearing the university crest, which they wear during the commencement ceremony and subsequently keep as a lifelong memento. The tradition originated in 1901, when the first cohort of post-Saito graduates sought a visual marker of their academic achievement distinct from the military uniforms that dominated public life at the time.

===Women's education===
Women were first admitted to OIU in 1946, following the post-war educational reforms. The first female graduate, {{Nihongo|Yōko Ishikawa|石川陽子|Ishikawa Yōko}}, completed her degree in medicine in 1950 and went on to establish the nation's first women's health clinic in Okami. Today, women constitute approximately 38% of the undergraduate student body and 42% of postgraduate students, though the figure varies significantly by faculty—women make up over 60% of the Faculty of Literature but only 15% of the Faculty of Engineering.

The university established the {{Nihongo|Office of Gender Equality|ジェンダー平等室|Jendā Byōdō-shitsu}} in 2012, following a high-profile incident in which a Faculty of Medicine professor was found to have systematically downgraded female students' examination scores. The office administers mentorship programs, childcare subsidies, and anti-harassment training, and has been credited with increasing the female faculty ratio from 8% in 2012 to 19% in 2026.

==Notable alumni==
{{Main|List of Okami Imperial University alumni}}
OIU has produced an extraordinary number of leaders across every sector of Sanese society. As of 2026, the university's alumni include:
* '''Prime Ministers:''' 9 of Okaiken's 22 post-Saito prime ministers were OIU graduates, including [[Honda Tadashige]] (1961–1964) and [[Toshiro Kurosawa]] (2013–2018)
* '''Imperial family:''' [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], who studied chemistry in the Faculty of Science and captained the university rugby team
* '''Military leaders:''' Several commanders of the [[Forces of Okaiken|Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] and Imperial-era military officers
* '''Scientists:''' {{Nihongo|Hidetoshi Nakamura|中村英利|Nakamura Hidetoshi}}, discoverer of the [[Kōryū Effect]]; {{Nihongo|Masahiro Tanaka|田中正弘|Tanaka Masahiro}}, pioneer of the [[Satori]] algorithmic framework
* '''Literary figures:''' {{Nihongo|Yukio Shimizu|清水雪雄|Shimizu Yukio}}, founder of the {{Nihongo|"Okami School"|大紙派|Ōkami-ha}} literary movement; {{Nihongo|Kazuo Takeda|武田和夫|Takeda Kazuo}}, novelist
* '''Business leaders:''' Numerous presidents of the [[zaibatsu]] conglomerates and modern technology companies
* '''Athletes:''' [[Naoto Kongo]], X-League middle linebacker and Rice Bowl MVP; {{Nihongo|Daiki Hoshino|星野大樹|Hoshino Daiki}}, Goriki competitor; numerous [[Okaiken Rugby League One|League One]] rugby players

